    "passage": "Hospice care refers to a specialized and compassionate form of medical attention and support provided to people with terminal illnesses and their family or caregivers.\nPatients on hospice care benefit from more [[1]] attention and services tailored to their needs. A dedicated team will attend to every patient in person, taking into account his or her personal preferences as well as medical history. Patients are usually offered effective management of [[2]] such as pain to ensure optimal comfort and well-being, and help with activities of daily living. Beyond the physical aspects, hospice care [[3]] spiritual support to patients while looking after their mental health. Skilled professionals provide one-on-one talks or counseling to help patients [[4]] the emotional challenges associated with end-of-life experiences.\nFamilies and caregivers also receive invaluable support and education. Respite options, for example, allow caregivers to take much-needed breaks lest they should be [[5]] and worn out. Subsequent services also assist them in coping with [[6]] after the patient’s passing or death. In a word, families of patients on hospice care can take comfort in the [[7]] shown to them by the caring medical staff and social workers throughout the process.\nSome, however, have problems with this concept due to cultural and religious beliefs. They may raise [[8]] regarding hastening death, or fear inadequate pain management. They may instead [[9]] more aggressive medical options or prefer to prolong life at any cost. Others are worried whether the care helps preserve patients’ [[10]] or self-esteem once they become hardly able to bathe or feed themselves. Whatever issues people may have, there’s no denying that hospice care is important, for both patients and their family, and is thus here to stay.",
